  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1008 PM CST TUE NOV 10 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery this evening reveals a shortwave moving\nacross Texas towards the southeast US. A batch of showers and\nstorms can be seen moving along the Texas coast towards the\nLouisiana border. If you look off to our northwest, you can see a\nstronger upper low beginning to dive across the Northern Plains.\nAs the shortwave moves our way through the day tomorrow, we will\nsee a gradual moistening of the mid to upper levels of the\natmosphere. We will continue with a low chance for light showers\nas this shortwave scoots through. Based on forecast soundings,\nthis could all very well be virga as the lower levels remain dry.\nThe northern trough quickly follows the southern shortwave,\nmoving across the Tennessee Valley Wednesday night through\nThursday. This system will kick a cold front our way late\nWednesday through Thursday morning. We will continue to carry low\nto medium chances for showers along the boundary with the best\nchances coming across our northern areas. There may be just\nenough instability for an isolated thunderstorm across our\nnorthern areas, but severe storms are not expected at this time.\nA much cooler air mass moves in behind the front, with highs in\nthe 50s and lows down into the mid 30s. A secondary surge of high\npressure brings a surge of even colder air to Central Alabama\nover the weekend which will bring high chances for the first\nfreeze of the season across northern portions of the area. Latest\nNBM probabilities maintain a 60-90% chance for freezing temps\nacross the northern half of the CWA. We start to warm back up\nearly next week with highs rebounding into the low 60s by\nTuesday.\n\n95/Castillo",
